<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="chp9sec3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Strategies for Computing the Determinant</title>

    <p>
        There are many ways to compute the determinant, all of which are equivalent. 
        In this lesson, I go over a few different strategies for computing the determinant.
        Depending on the situation, some are more advantageous than others.
    </p>

	<p>
        The primary method that I recommend for computing the determinant is to use <m>LU</m>-decompositions.  By  <xref ref="prop-triangulardet" /> we can easily compute the determinants of <m>L</m> and <m>U</m>.  By the determinant product formula,  <xref ref="thm-detproductformula" />, we get <m>\det(A)=\det(L)\det(U)</m>.  Furthermore, when <m>L</m> has 1's along the diagonal (as is the case when we use the Doolittle Algorithm), then <m>\det(A)= u_1\cdots u_n</m> where <m>u_1, \ldots, u_n</m> are the diagonal entries of <m>U</m>.
        If <m>A</m> does not have an <m>LU</m>-decomposition, it still has a <m>PLU</m>-decomposition, where the <m>P</m> just introduces additional reflections, and hence the general formula is:
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="prop-det_plu">
        <title><m>PLU</m>-Equation for the Determinant</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>A</m> is an <m>n\times n</m> matrix with <m>PLU</m>-decomposition <m>A=PLU</m> coming from the Doolittle Algorithm, then 
            </p>
            <men xml:id="eq-det_plu">
                \det(A) = \sgn(P)u_1\cdots u_n.
            </men>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <example>
        <title>Using the <m>PLU</m>-Equation</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>A= \begin{bmatrix}1 \amp 2 \amp 3\\4 \amp 5 \amp 6\\7 \amp 8 \amp 9\end{bmatrix}</m>, then using Type 3 row operations to clear down,
            </p>
            <md>
				<mrow>
                \left[\begin{array}{rrr}1 \amp 2 \amp 3\\4 \amp 5 \amp 6\\7 \amp 8 \amp 9\end{array}\right]
                \rowA{2}{1} {-4}
                \left[\begin{array}{rrr}1 \amp 2 \amp 3\\0 \amp -3 \amp -6\\7 \amp 8 \amp 9\end{array}\right]
				</mrow>
				<mrow>
                \rowA{3}{1} {-7}
                \left[\begin{array}{rrr}1 \amp 2 \amp 3\\0 \amp -3 \amp -6\\0 \amp -6 \amp -12\end{array}\right]
				</mrow>
				<mrow>
                \rowA{3}{2} {-2}
                \left[\begin{array}{rrr}1 \amp 2 \amp 3\\0 \amp -3 \amp -6\\0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right],
				</mrow>
            </md>
            <p>
                so <xref ref="eq-det_plu"/> gives
            </p>
            <me>\det(A) = (1)(-3)(0)=0.</me>
        </statement>
    </example>

    <p>
        This <m>LU</m>-decomposition strategy can be used to derive the determinant formulas for <m>2\times 2 </m> and <m>3\times 3</m> matrices.    
        These formulas are well known and you are likely to see them in situations outside of this course.   
        I will derive both now.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="prop-2x2det_formula">
        <title><m>2\times 2</m> Determinant Formula</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>\begin{vmatrix}a \amp b\\c \amp d\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <proof>
        <p>
            The proof follows from two cases: <m>a\ne 0</m> and <m>a=0</m>.  I will show the former and leave the latter for you to investigate.
            If <m>a \ne 0</m>, then
        </p>
        <me>
            \left[\begin{array}{cc}
            a \amp b\\
            c \amp d
            \end{array}\right]
            \rowA{2}{1}{-\frac{c}{a}}
            \left[\begin{array}{cc}
            a \amp b\\
            0 \amp d-\frac{bc}{a}
            \end{array}\right]
            =
            \left[\begin{array}{cc}
            a \amp b\\
            0 \amp \frac{ad-bc}{a}
            \end{array}\right]
        </me>
        <p>
            Then, using the Doolittle Algorithm, the associated <m>LU</m>-decomposition is then
        </p>
        <me>
            \left[\begin{array}{cc}
            a \amp b\\
            c \amp d
            \end{array}\right]
            =
            \left[\begin{array}{cc}
            1 \amp 0\\
            \frac{c}{a} \amp 1
            \end{array}\right]
            \left[\begin{array}{cc}
            a \amp b\\
            0 \amp \frac{ad-bc}{a}
            \end{array}\right]
            .
        </me>
        <p>
            Using Equation <xref ref="eq-det_plu"/>,
        </p>
        <me>\begin{vmatrix}a \amp b\\c \amp d\end{vmatrix}= a\left(\frac{ad-bc}{a}\right)=ad-bc.</me>
    </proof>

    <p>
        A useful mnemonic to remember these two terms is to visualize this as adding the downward (<m>\textcolor{blue}{blue}</m>) diagonal and subtracting the upward (<m>\textcolor{red}{red}</m>) diagonal.   
    </p>

	<image source="images/9.3_2x2.png" width="20%"/>

	<!--
    <me>
        \begin{bNiceMatrix}[columns-width = 7mm]
        a \amp b\\
        c \amp d\\
        \CodeAfter   
          \tikz \draw [opacity=.4,line width=3.5mm,line cap=round, color=blue] 
                        (1-1.center) - (2-2.center);
          \tikz \draw [opacity=.4,line width=3.5mm,line cap=round, color=red] 
                        (2-1.center) - (1-2.center);
        \end{bNiceMatrix}
    </me>
	-->



    <theorem xml:id="prop-3x3det_formula">
        <title><m>3\times 3</m> Determinant Formula</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                <m>\begin{vmatrix}a \amp b \amp c\\d \amp e \amp f\\g \amp h \amp i\end{vmatrix}= aei+bfg+cdh-ceg-afh-bdi</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <proof>
        <p>
            The proof follows from a few cases.  I will assume <m>a\ne 0</m> and <m>ae-bd\ne0</m> and leave the other cases for you to investigate.
            If <m>a \ne 0</m>, then
        </p>
        <md>
            <mrow>
            \left[\begin{array}{ccc}
            a \amp b \amp c\\d \amp e \amp f\\g \amp h \amp i
            \end{array}\right]
            \amp\rowA{2}{1}{-\frac{d}{a}}
            \left[\begin{array}{ccc}
            a \amp b \amp c\\
            0 \amp e-\frac{bd}{a} \amp f-\frac{cd}{a}\\
            g \amp h \amp i
            \end{array}\right]
			</mrow>
			<mrow>
            \amp \rowA{3}{1}{-\frac{g}{a}}
            \left[\begin{array}{ccc}
            a \amp b \amp c\\
            0 \amp e-\frac{bd}{a} \amp f-\frac{cd}{a}\\
            0 \amp h-\frac{bg}{a} \amp i-\frac{cg}{a}
            \end{array}\right]
            =
            \left[\begin{array}{ccc}
            a \amp b \amp c\\
            0 \amp \frac{ae-bd}{a} \amp \frac{af-cd}{a}\\
            0 \amp \frac{ah-bg}{a} \amp \frac{ai-cg}{a}
            \end{array}\right]
			</mrow>
			<mrow>
            \amp \rowA{3}{2}{-\frac{ah-bg}{ae-bd}}
            \left[\begin{array}{ccc}
            a \amp b \amp c\\
            0 \amp \frac{ae-bd}{a} \amp \frac{af-cd}{a}\\
            0 \amp 0 \amp \frac{ai-cg}{a}-\left(\frac{af-cd}{a}\right)\left(\frac{ah-bg}{ae-bd}\right)
            \end{array}\right].
            </mrow>
        </md>
        <p>
            Observe that the bottom right term can be algebraically simplified:
        </p>
        <me>
            \begin{aligned}
            \frac{ai-cg}{a}-\left(\frac{af-cd}{a}\right)\left(\frac{ah-bg}{ae-bd}\right)
            \amp=
            \frac{(ai-cg)(ae-bd)-(af-cd)(ah-bg)}{a(ae-bd)}\\
            \amp=
            \frac{(a^2ei-aceg-abdi+bcdg)-(a^2fh-acdh-abfg+bcdg)}{a(ae-bd)}\\
            \amp=
            \frac{aei+bfg+cdh-ceg-afh-bdi}{ae-bd}.
            \end{aligned}
        </me>
        <p>
            Then, using the Doolittle Algorithm, the associated <m>LU</m>-decomposition is then
        </p>
        <me>
            \left[\begin{array}{ccc}
            a \amp b \amp c\\d \amp e \amp f\\g \amp h \amp i
            \end{array}\right]
            =
            \left[\begin{array}{ccc}
            1 \amp 0 \amp 0\\ \frac{d}{a} \amp 1 \amp 0\\ \frac{g}{a} \amp \frac{ah-bg}{ae-bd} \amp 1
            \end{array}\right]
            \left[\begin{array}{ccc}
            a \amp b \amp c\\
            0 \amp \frac{ae-bd}{a} \amp \frac{af-cd}{a}\\
            0 \amp 0 \amp \frac{aei+bfg+cdh-ceg-afh-bdi}{ae-bd}
            \end{array}\right]
            .
        </me>
        <p>
            Using Equation <xref ref="prop-det_plu"/>,
        </p>
        <md>
			<mrow>
            \begin{vmatrix}a \amp b \amp c\\d \amp e \amp f\\g \amp h \amp i\end{vmatrix} 
			\amp= a\left(\frac{ae-bd}{a}\right)\left(\frac{aei+bfg+cdh-ceg-afh-bdi}{ae-bd}\right)
			</mrow>
			<mrow>
			\amp= aei+bfg+cdh-ceg-afh-bdi.
			</mrow>
        </md>
    </proof>

    <p>
        A useful mnemonic to remember these six terms is the <em>Rule of Sarrus</em>, which visualizes this as adding the following three downward (<m>\textcolor{blue}{blue}</m>) diagonals and subtracting the following three upward (<m>\textcolor{red}{red}</m>) diagonals.   
    </p>

	<image source="images/9.3_3x3.png" width="50%"/>

	<!--
    <me>
        \begin{NiceMatrix}[columns-width = 7mm]
        a \amp b \amp c \amp a \amp b\\
        d \amp e \amp f \amp d \amp e\\
        g \amp h \amp i \amp g \amp h\\
        \CodeAfter [delimiters/color=black]
          \SubMatrix[{1-1}{3-3}]
          \tikz \draw [opacity=.4,line width=3.5mm,line cap=round, color=blue] 
                        (1-1.center) - (3-3.center)
                        (1-2.center) - (3-4.center)
                        (1-3.center) - (3-5.center) ;
          \tikz \draw [opacity=.4,line width=4mm,line cap=round, color=red] 
                        (3-1.center) - (1-3.center)
                        (3-2.center) - (1-4.center)
                        (3-3.center) - (1-5.center) ;
        \end{NiceMatrix}
    </me>
	-->


    <p>
        There are no similar determinant formulas for <m>n\times n</m> matrices with <m>n\ge 4</m>.
        However, there is another well-known strategy for computing determinants, which is a recursive process known as <em>cofactor expansion</em>.  Even though I do not recommend cofactor expansion in general, I include it here as you are likely to see it outside of this course and I want to make sure you are prepared accordingly.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-cofactor">
        <title>Cofactor</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>A</m> is an <m>n\times n</m> matrix and <m>i,j\in \{1,\ldots, n\}</m>, then let <m>A_{ij}</m> denote the <m>(n-1)\times (n-1)</m> matrix obtained by removing the <m>i^{th}</m> row and <m>j^{th}</m> column of <m>A</m>.  The number <m>C_{ij}=(-1)^{i+j}\det(A_{ij})</m> the <m>ij^{th}</m> <term>cofactor</term> of <m>A</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>A= \begin{bmatrix}1 \amp 2 \amp 3\\4 \amp 5 \amp 6\\7 \amp 8 \amp 9\end{bmatrix}</m>, then 
                <m>A_{11} = \begin{bmatrix} \rst{1} \amp \rst{2} \amp \rst{3}\\ \rst{4} \amp 5 \amp 6\\ \rst{7} \amp 8 \amp 9\end{bmatrix}</m>
                and its cofactor is <m>C_{11}=(-1)^2\begin{vmatrix}5 \amp 6\\8 \amp 9\end{vmatrix}=-3</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>

    <theorem xml:id="prop-cofactor_expansion">
        <title>Cofactor Expansion</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>A</m> is an <m>n\times n</m> matrix, then 
            </p>
            <men xml:id="eq-det_cofactor">
                \det(A)=a_{i1}(-1)^{i+1}\det(A_{i1})+a_{i2}(-1)^{i+2}\det(A_{i2})+\cdots +a_{in}(-1)^{i+n}\det(A_{in}).
            </men>
            <p>
                We call this the <alert>cofactor expansion of <m>\det(A)</m> along the <m>i^{th}</m> row of <m>A</m></alert>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <proof>
        <title>Sketch of Proof of Proposition <xref ref="prop-cofactor_expansion"/></title>
        <p>
            This proof is technical and I will leave some details to the reader.  Furthermore, I will only show the cofactor expansion for the first row, as other rows can be obtained by a row permutation.  
            Write <m>A=[\vect{v}_1|\vect{v}_2|\cdots |\vect{v}_n]=[a_{ij}]</m> and let <m>\vect{w}_i</m> denote the list-length <m>(n-1)</m> vector of entries <m>2</m> through <m>n</m> of <m>\vect{v}_i</m>.       
            We know that Type 3 column operations, clearing right, do not affect the determinant.  
            Then using column notation, 
        </p>
        <men xml:id="eq-cofactor_proof">
            \det(A) \amp = \det(\vect{v}_1,\vect{v}_2,\ldots, \vect{v}_n)\\
            \amp =\det\left(\vect{v}_1,\vect{v}_2-\frac{a_{12}}{a_{11}}\vect{v}_1,\ldots, \vect{v}_n-\frac{a_{1n}}{a_{11}}\vect{v}_1\right)\\
            \amp =a_{11}\det\left(\vect{w}_2-\frac{a_{12}}{a_{11}}\vect{w}_1,\ldots, \vect{w}_n-\frac{a_{1n}}{a_{11}}\vect{w}_1\right)\\
            \amp =a_{11}\left(\det\left(\vect{w}_2-\frac{a_{12}}{a_{11}}\vect{w}_1,\ldots, \vect{w}_n-\frac{a_{1n}}{a_{11}}\vect{w}_1\right)\right).
        </men>
        <p>
            Using the additive and alternating properties of the determinant (see Problem <xref ref="problem-det_alternating"/> and Problem <xref ref="problem-det_additivity"/>), it can be shown that this last expression <xref ref="eq-cofactor_proof"/> reduces to the cofactor expansion <xref ref="eq-det_cofactor"/>.   
        </p>
    </proof>

    <example>
        <title>Using Cofactor Expansion</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>A= \begin{bmatrix}1 \amp 2 \amp 3\\4 \amp 5 \amp 6\\7 \amp 8 \amp 9\end{bmatrix}</m>, then the cofactor expansion of <m>\det(A)</m> along the first row of <m>A</m> is:
            </p>
            <me>
                \begin{aligned}
                \det(A)\amp=a_{11}C_{11}+a_{12}C_{12}+a_{13}C_{13}\\
                \amp= (+1)(1) 
				\begin{vmatrix} \rst{1} \amp \rst{2} \amp \rst{3}\\ \rst{4} \amp 5 \amp 6\\ \rst{7} \amp 8 \amp 9\end{vmatrix}
				+ (-1)(2) 
				\begin{vmatrix} \rst{1} \amp \rst{2} \amp \rst{3}\\ 4 \amp \rst{5} \amp 6\\ 7 \amp \rst{8} \amp 9\end{vmatrix}
                + (+1)(3) 
				\begin{vmatrix} \rst{1} \amp \rst{2} \amp \rst{3}\\ 4 \amp 5 \amp \rst{6}\\ 7 \amp 8 \amp \rst{9}\end{vmatrix}
                \\
                \amp=+1(45-48) -2(36-42)+3(32-35)\\
                \amp=-3+12-9\\
                \amp=0.
                \end{aligned}
            </me>
        </statement>
    </example>

    <p>
        Cofactor expansion is nice in that it recursive, and hence may be easily coded.  It is conceptually nice in that it reduces a big computation into <m>n</m> many smaller ones.  One can easily use this procedure to compute determinants by hand for small <m>n</m>, and the checkerboard pattern of <m>(-1)^{i+j}</m> is useful for correctly working out the signs.  This is the method often taught in multivariable calculus, in particular for computing cross products.  
    </p>

    <p>
        In fact, cofactor expansion is often given as the definition of the determinant.  
        That being said, this method has multiple deficiencies:
    </p>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <p>the connection between this method and the geometric intuition for the determinant is not easy to see.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>For large <m>n</m>, our Doolittle Algorithm (and variants) are far more efficient than cofactor expansion.  The Doolittle Algorithm takes roughly <m>n^3</m> many algebraic operations, whereas cofactor expansion take roughly <m>n!</m> many algebraic operations.</p>
        </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
        You should be able to compute by hand determinants for all <m>2\times 2</m>, <m>3\times 3</m>, and some particularly simple larger matrices.  You should know hot to use technology to compute determinants too, both the double check your computations and to compute the determinants for larger matrices.  
        GeoGebra will compute the determinant of a matrix using the <c>Determinant</c> command.  
    </p>

    <question>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Compute the determinant for each of the following matrices.  Try using both the <m>PLU</m>-equation and cofactor expansion.  Compare and contrast the two methods.  Then double check your computations with GeoGebra.  
            </p>
            <me>
                \left[\begin{array}{rrr}1 \amp 1 \amp 1\\1 \amp 2 \amp 2\\1 \amp 2 \amp 3\end{array}\right]
                \hspace{2pc}
                \left[\begin{array}{rrr}1 \amp 2 \amp 4\\3 \amp 7 \amp 15\\2 \amp 8 \amp 21\end{array}\right]
                \hspace{2pc}
                \left[\begin{array}{rrr}0 \amp 2 \amp 9\\4 \amp 3 \amp 7\\2 \amp 1 \amp 2\end{array}\right]
                \hspace{2pc}
            </me>
        </statement>
    </question>






<exercises>
    <title>Problems</title>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>A = \left[\begin{array}{rr}7 \amp 5\\2 \amp 4\end{array}\right]</m>.
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li><p>Find an elementary matrix decomposition of <m>A</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>Use this decomposition to find the area scaling factor of <m>A</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>Use this decomposition to find the sign of <m>A</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>Deduce <m>\det(A)</m>.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
            Consider the matrix <m>A=\left[\begin{array}{rrr}2 \amp 1 \amp 4\\-2 \amp 0 \amp -1\\-4 \amp -5 \amp -12\end{array}\right]</m>.
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li><p>Find an <m>LU</m>-decomposition of <m>A</m>.  Do this by hand.</p></li>
            <li><p>Using your decomposition, compute <m>\det(A)</m>.</p></li>
        </ol>
        </statement>
    </exercise>



    <exercise>
      <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>A</m> be an <m>n\times n</m> matrix and <m>A=PBP^{-1}</m> be a similar matrix decomposition.
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li><p>In complete sentences, using geometric words, what is the geometric <em>MEANING</em> of the fact that <m>\det(A)=\det(B)</m>, in the context of coordinates?</p></li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Rigorously algebraically prove <m>\det(A)=\det(B)</m>.  Clearly state each definition and algebraic property of sign and volume scaling factor you use.  
                        Be careful to present your arguments in complete sentences and with a clear logical progression.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
    </exercise>



    <exercise>
      <statement>

    <p>
        Suppose you have the following three matrices <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m> with the given decompositions (elementary, <m>LU</m>, and similar, respectively).  For each, 
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li><p>Use the decomposition to compute the determinant of the given matrix by hand with minimal computations.</p></li>
        <li><p>Explain: what you are looking at to minimize your computations (and what can you ignore)?</p></li>
        <li><p>Reflect upon how matrix decompositions can be <em>used</em> to make the problem of computing determinant simple.</p></li>
    </ol>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                <m>A=\left[\begin{array}{rr} 1 \amp 6\\0 \amp 1\end{array}\right]
                \left[\begin{array}{rr} 5 \amp 0\\0 \amp 1\end{array}\right]
                \left[\begin{array}{rr} 0 \amp 1\\1 \amp 0\end{array}\right]
                \left[\begin{array}{rr} 1 \amp 0\\0 \amp -2\end{array}\right]
                \left[\begin{array}{rr} 1 \amp 0\\-7 \amp 1\end{array}\right]
                \left[\begin{array}{rr} 1 \amp 9\\0 \amp 1\end{array}\right]
                \left[\begin{array}{rr} 1 \amp 0\\12 \amp 1\end{array}\right]
                \left[\begin{array}{rr} 3 \amp 0\\0 \amp 1\end{array}\right]</m>
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <m>B = \left[\begin{array}{rrr}1 \amp 0 \amp 0\\2 \amp 1 \amp 0\\3 \amp 2 \amp 1\end{array}\right]
                \left[\begin{array}{rrr}5 \amp 1 \amp 7\\0 \amp 1 \amp 8\\0 \amp 0 \amp 3\end{array}\right]</m>
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <m>C = \left[\begin{array}{rrr}3 \amp 7 \amp 1\\4 \amp 5 \amp 2\\1 \amp 2 \amp 7\end{array}\right]
                \left[\begin{array}{rrr}3 \amp 0 \amp 0\\0 \amp 2 \amp 0\\0 \amp 0 \amp 1\end{array}\right]
                \left[\begin{array}{rrr}3 \amp 7 \amp 1\\4 \amp 5 \amp 2\\1 \amp 2 \amp 7\end{array}\right]^{-1}</m>
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
        </statement>
    </exercise>



  <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
            If <m>A</m> is a square matrix, show that <m>\det(A^T)=\det(A)</m>.  (Hint: You might find it useful to use the Sock and Shoes property of transpose (Proposition ) and an appropriately chosen matrix decomposition.)
        </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>



    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
            If <m>A</m> is a square matrix with orthonormal columns, show that <m>\det(A)=1 \text{ or } -1</m>. 
        </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>\vect{v},\vect{w}\in \R^2</m> and <m>a\in \R</m>, give a geometric proof of the following scaling property of the determinant: <m>\det(a\vect{v},\vect{w})=a\det(\vect{v},\vect{w})</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
            If <m>S=\{\vect{v}_1,\ldots,\vect{v}_n\} \subset \R^n</m> is a basis and <m>a\in \R</m>, give an algebraic proof of the following scaling property of the determinant: 
        </p>
        <me>
            \det(a\vect{v}_1,\vect{v}_2,,\ldots ,\vect{v}_n)=\det(a\vect{v}_1,\vect{v}_2,\ldots ,\vect{v}_n).
        </me>
        </statement>
    </exercise>


  <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
            If <m>\vect{v},\vect{w}\in \R^2</m>, give a geometric proof of the following skew-symmetric property of the determinant: <m>\det(\vect{v},\vect{w})=-\det(\vect{w},\vect{v})</m>.
        </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>


     <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>S=\{\vect{v}_1,\ldots,\vect{v}_n\} \subset \R^n</m>, give an algebraic proof of the following skew-symmetric property of the determinant: 
            </p>
            <me>
                \det(\vect{v}_1,\vect{v}_2,\vect{v}_3,\ldots ,\vect{v}_n)=-\det(\vect{v}_2,\vect{v}_1,\vect{v}_3,\ldots ,\vect{v}_n).
            </me>
        </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise xml:id="problem-det_alternating">
      <statement>
        <p >
            If <m>S=\{\vect{v}_1,\ldots,\vect{v}_n\} \subset \R^n</m>, give an algebraic proof of the following alternating property of the determinant: 
        </p>
        <me>
            \det(\vect{v}_1,\vect{v}_1,\vect{v}_3,\ldots ,\vect{v}_n)=0.
        </me>
        </statement>
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
      <statement>

        <p>
            If <m>\vect{u},\vect{v},\vect{w}\in \R^2</m>, give a geometric proof of the following additive property of the determinant: <m>\det(\vect{u}+\vect{v},\vect{w})=\det(\vect{u},\vect{w})+\det(\vect{v},\vect{w})</m>.
        </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>


  <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p xml:id="problem-det_additivity">
            If <m>S=\{\vect{v}_1,\ldots,\vect{v}_n\} \subset \R^n</m> is a basis and <m>\vect{w}\in \R^n</m>, give an algebraic proof of the following additive property of the determinant: 
        </p>
        <me>
            \det(\vect{v}_1+\vect{w},\vect{v}_2,\ldots ,\vect{v}_n)=\det(\vect{v}_1,\vect{v}_2,\ldots ,\vect{v}_n)+\det(\vect{w},\vect{v}_2,\ldots ,\vect{v}_n).
        </me>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

</exercises>



</section>